# ENLIGHTENED: THE ACADEMY ERA, BOOK 1, “THE KING AND THE CLIMB”

## Chapter Twenty-One: Second Blood

*“******Every winner was, until that morning, everyone else in the room.******”*

*Hiopi ritual-day proverb, reproduced in the Year 1 cultural packet*

The bread has been in the bay overnight and it tastes like the bay.

“It’s fine,” Denic says.

“It tastes like grease,” I say.

“It tastes like the tin,” Denic says. “The tin’s clean.”

“The tin sat next to the grease,” Doyle says, from across the table, not looking up. “I told you not to put it in the bay.”

“You said don’t put it in the bay,” Denic says. “You didn’t say what happens.”

“What happens is this,” Doyle says.

I take another piece anyway, because there’s most of a loaf of it and it’s the white one with the crust that comes off in a single sheet, and even through the grease the crust is the crust.

“Four,” Fenn says.

“Four what,” Denic says.

“Four rolls of wrap,” Fenn says. “That’s what he paid. He’s been telling people all week and he hasn’t once said the number, so I’m saying it.”

“It was three,” Denic says.

“It was four,” Fenn says. “Petra saw the third one go and then saw the fourth one go on Sunday.”

“The fourth one was separate,” Denic says.

“The fourth one was for the tin,” Petra says, delighted, leaning across Ilyen’s tray to get an angle on him.

Corw comes down the bench with his tray already squared and sits and doesn’t start on any of it.

“Thirteen years, seven days,” Corw says.

“Corw,” I say.

“Seven days and you have not said one word about it,” Corw says. “Not one. You had a whole Friday to yourself last week and you spent it the same as every other Friday, and then you had the weekend and you spent that in the bay, and nobody has heard you say the number out loud once.”

“There’s no number to say,” I say.

“There is a number,” Corw says. “The number is thirteen. That’s the only interesting number a person gets to have this year and you’re sitting on it.”

“You gave him the number,” Fenn says.

“I gave him the count,” Corw says. “That’s a service.”

“That’s what you got him,” Fenn says.

Corw looks at her for a second and then starts eating.

“You had eleven days,” Doyle says, to Denic, not looking up from his own tray. “The eleven days were in June.”

“I know when the eleven days were,” Denic says.

“Then you know they’re gone,” Doyle says.

“I got there,” Denic says.

“You got there a week late,” Doyle says.

“It’s the same bread either way,” Denic says.

Nobody explains any of that to me and I don’t ask. I get another piece before it’s gone.

Doyle has the notices out and the stack is thick enough now that he has to press it flat with his palm before the top one will lie down. He goes over each of them twice with his lips moving before any of it comes out.

“Thirteen,” Doyle says.

“Twenty-one,” Corw says.

“Twenty-one is not a real number,” Doyle says. “Twenty-one is my thirteen and your eight that nobody read.”

“Eight went up,” Corw says. “Eight is eight whether anybody read it.”

“Then say eight,” Doyle says.

“I say twenty-one,” Corw says, and Doyle folds the top notice back along its crease and moves on to the second one without answering that.

Denic’s got the peg board out flat and he’s running nine to five with the left hand, and the left hand goes nine, seven, five, and doesn’t stop in the middle.

“There,” Denic says.

“That’s the third one this morning,” Ilyen says, from the end.

“It’s the fourth,” Denic says.

“Third,” Ilyen says. “The first one you went around.”

“Going around still gets there,” Denic says.

“It gets there,” Ilyen says, and eats one item all the way to the end of it before she starts the next.

Petra says something into Ilyen’s shoulder that none of the rest of us get, and Ilyen makes the short noise that comes out of her nose, and Petra puts her whole hand over her own mouth and holds it there.

Denic looks up from the board just long enough to see it and goes back to the diagonal without saying anything.

Reqa comes in on the tail of it with her sleeves down for once and her hands already black to the second knuckle. It’s half six. The bay’s had her since before the hall opened.

“Straps,” Reqa says.

“I’ve got mine,” I say.

“I know you’ve got yours,” Reqa says. “I’m telling you the fourth one’s going to start doing it again if you keep putting your elbow into it. There’s nothing to pull against now and you’re still pulling.”

“I’m not still pulling,” I say.

“You did it Tuesday,” Reqa says, and takes half my bread, hers being wherever hers goes, and stops with it halfway up. “What is this.”

“It’s the good one,” Denic says.

“It’s been somewhere,” Reqa says.

“It’s been in the bay,” Doyle says.

Reqa eats it anyway and doesn’t say a second word about it, and I get the one I’ve had since yesterday out and try it on her.

“That’s the seventh machine,” I say. “Wrap goes in one end. Bread comes out the other. Takes eleven days.”

Reqa doesn’t laugh. Her mouth goes at one corner and stays there for about a second before she puts it away, and she goes back to the bread.

“There’s no seventh,” Corw says.

“There’s a seventh now,” Fenn says.

“That’s not how machines work,” Doyle says.

The corridor out to the arena wing runs downhill for the last hundred metres and the doors at the bottom stand open, and the air coming up through them in the third week of June is warmer than it was in May and carries more of the turned dirt in it. The grit gets into the stairwell now. It didn’t in February.

The shed queue moves the way it moves. My trainer’s second from the end where the shorter cockpits sit. The kit-check board is up by the door with the row assignments on it, and I get my own row off the number and off where my name sits in the block, and the two lines under it I get about half of and don’t stop for.

Up the ladder. Four straps, twelve and stop, twelve and stop. The fourth one goes through clean and my elbow stays where it is.

The collar comes forward off the seatback and settles at the base of my skull and the panel lights its row of shapes. My hands go to the rests and close.

“Pick your weapon and face it,” the line says, through a thousand collars at once.

The first one is a stranger who holds too much of his weight on the front foot and I have him before he’s finished stepping. The second is a girl I’ve had twice before and don’t know the name of, and she goes six exchanges and then goes down. The third is over quick enough that I’m back in my square with the four seconds running and the square’s still warm from the last time I stood in it.

That’s the difference this morning, and it isn’t the four seconds. The four seconds are the four seconds, the same gap the line has always left. It’s how many of them there are. One goes down and the line takes him and there’s another one stood in front of me before my arm’s back, and by the end of the first hour I’ve been through more of them than a whole morning gave me in March. My arms are hot already. In March they didn’t get hot until the fourth.

Vantree comes down the return line the way she comes down it every Friday, unhurried, stopping at maybe one square in forty. She stops at mine.

The canopy’s up. She’s standing at the foot of the ladder with the sun behind her and she waits for me to get it all the way back before she says anything.

“Calder,” Vantree says.

“Instructor,” I say.

“Your back foot’s set,” Vantree says.

“You said four hundred times,” I say.

“I said four hundred and you did four hundred,” Vantree says. “Now you’re standing on it. You set it and then you wait on it, and you’re waiting about a tenth longer than the foot needs, because you’re still checking it’s there.”

“How do I stop checking,” I say.

“You stop checking when there’s something else worth looking at,” Vantree says, already moving. “Stop watching your own feet and start watching where his weight sits. That’s the next one.”

She’s two squares down before the canopy’s back and she doesn’t come back and say it again.

I lose the next one. Not because of the foot.

Somewhere in the second hour the field goes the way it goes, four hundred squares of it running the same rhythm, and I stop having a count.

Then the four seconds run and the square across from me fills with something down on four legs.

He comes off the line the way he came off it in March, low and long, torso and two arms sitting up over the front end of him, the whole shape of him looking half folded up even standing still.

“Calder,” Vrassik says.

“Vrassik,” I say.

“The line’s put me down beside you four times this morning,” Vrassik says. “I watched you take the one with the reach. He did that to me twenty minutes ago and it took me two goes.”

“It took me one,” I say.

“I am aware of how many it took you,” Vrassik says, and the lance comes off his mount already moving.

He doesn’t wait for the line and he never has. All four legs drive at once, no rock, no tell, and the ground between us stops being ground I can use.

I go before I’ve decided to.

The step is already out and the back foot is already down and the whole committed weight of him goes past my left shoulder close enough that the wash of it turns me, and the seam’s where the seam has always been, the half second after a charge where four legs have to gather before they can turn, and my tip is in the shoulder joint before he’s got the second leg back under him.

He goes over. It’s not close.

The panel calls it and the line takes him and I stand in an empty square for four seconds with my arm still out.

The line puts him back in the square two down and his canopy comes up before mine does, and the collar carries him in flat and close the way it carries everything else.

“That was faster,” Vrassik says.

“It was the same,” I say.

“It was not the same,” Vrassik says. “I was there for the first one. In March you looked at me and then you moved. There was a whole piece in the middle where you were looking.” He puts the lance back up on the mount. “There isn’t one now.”

“There’s one,” I say.

“There isn’t,” Vrassik says, and drops the canopy, and the colour along the hide of his forearm has climbed up out of the shoulder seam and is sitting there, lit, the same warm shade it went in March.

We go again inside the hour, and this time he doesn’t charge.

He comes down off the line and settles on all four and stops, lance up, forty degrees off square to me, and does nothing at all. I hold where I am and he holds where he is and the field goes on being loud around the pair of us.

There’s nothing coming off him. No weight shifting, no leg loading, nothing for a hand to go to, a big animal stood in dirt with its head up.

I go in on him because he isn’t going to, and he’s been waiting on that.

The turn comes from the back two legs and it comes the wrong way round, into me instead of away, and the tip catches under my right arm where the plate seams and it doesn’t glance. It goes in. He drives it and then he lifts, because four legs give him something under it that two never could, and my whole side goes off like a struck bell.

The ground gets me sideways.

There’s a lot of it. That’s the piece I get first, before the pain arrives properly, the amount, red running down the inside of the glass in front of me where it has no business being at all, and my own hand going flat against my side and not stopping it, and the heat coming up out of the middle of me and going out along both arms at once.

The panel calls it in under a second. It doesn’t give me the four.

The seat lets go. The collar goes cold at the base of my skull and everything else goes with it, the weight out of my stomach, the noise off the field, the arm, all of it dropping out at once like a hand coming off a switch, and then there’s the return and the cold running in through the collar and along the whole right side and into the hole.

It itches. That’s all it is. It goes cold and then it goes hot and then it itches like something biting, and there’s a taste at the back of my teeth like a coin, and the new skin under my kit comes up a different temperature than the rest of me and stays that way for about a minute.

Four seconds. The square. The lance in the mount at my side, fresh, the same as the one I dropped. My hands are shaking on the rests. Not much. They stop.

The third one, I walk at him.

He’s in the square across and he does the same nothing, four legs planted, lance up, waiting on me to come at him fast so he can turn into it again.

So I come slow. I put the lance down along my side and walk straight up the middle of the square at a pace you’d cross a mess hall at, and there’s nothing in it for him to read because there’s nothing in it. Ten metres. Eight. He has to do something.

He does it at six, and it’s the charge, because at six metres a standing start is the only answer four legs have, and the second his front two come up off the dirt he is a shape I have had a hundred times.

I’m already off the line of it.

He gets me on the way past. The shaft catches me across the hip and turns me most of the way round and my feet go and I take three long steps sideways to keep them, and it doesn’t matter, because he is committed and going and the seam opens behind him the way it opens every single time.

I put the tip into the shoulder joint from underneath and I put my weight up through it.

The near foreleg goes out from under him and the whole front of him comes down and the dirt takes it, and the panel calls it, and the two of us are lying about four metres apart with the field running over us and the line already coming for him.

The canopy comes up on its own. Vrassik gets his own up and doesn’t climb out and neither do I. He’s got one arm hanging off the rim of his cockpit and the hide along it has gone the colour it goes.

“You walked in,” Vrassik says.

“You weren’t moving,” I say.

“I was not moving on purpose,” Vrassik says. “I’ve had that one since March.”

“It worked,” I say.

“It worked once,” Vrassik says.

The line takes him while he’s still got his mouth open and puts him back in his own square, and he comes up out of it still talking, and nothing of it carries across. He waves at me until I get my own collar back on.

“Two to one,” Vrassik says.

“What,” I say.

“Two you’ve had off me today and one I’ve had off you,” Vrassik says. “I’m keeping it. I’m keeping all of them. In March it was nothing to nothing and now it’s two to one and there is a Friday every week from here to whenever they stop giving us Fridays.”

“That’s a long count,” I say.

“It’s a count,” Vrassik says, and drops the canopy and goes.

The rest of it runs the way the rest of it runs. Four hours is four hours. I lose two more and take four and the last one goes forty seconds against somebody who fights like he’s already worked out he’s going to lose it and wants it to take a while.

Corw’s past the gate with the band already up.

“Nineteen,” Corw says.

“Nineteen what,” I say.

“Yours,” Corw says. “I had you at nineteen and I lost you for about ten minutes in the middle so it’s nineteen or twenty. I had sixteen.” He falls in beside me without asking, the way he does. “Nineteen at a hundred and twenty is two thousand two hundred and eighty out of one morning.”

“You do that in your head?” I say.

“I do it while it’s happening,” Corw says. “That’s the only place it’s worth doing.”

Denic catches us at the top of the stairs coming the other way, against the flow, coming up from the board end of the corridor.

“I’m on it,” Denic says.

“On what,” Corw says.

“The board. The handling one,” Denic says, already turning around and going back up with us. “There’s a column on the board and it’s got six names in it and one of them is mine and four of the others are third years.”

“There is no handling column,” Corw says.

“Come and look at the handling column,” Denic says.

The board’s in the corridor outside the arena wing with its own light over it, and there’s nobody in front of it.

It’s a lot of names in a lot of rows with columns running off them, and I can find where mine sits because I know the shape of it and the shape hasn’t moved since March. The last letter of my own name is one of the two holes. I have known that since about week three and it has never once got in the way of finding it.

What I don’t have is the columns. Five headers run across the top of it, two words each, and two of the five have one of my two holes sitting in the middle, the second header and the fourth. That’s the whole of what reading gets me up there. The five itself I’d have had just as easy counting blind.

Corw goes straight down the right-hand side with his finger about a centimetre off the surface.

“Voss, D,” Corw says. “Extended-duration handling assessment. Tuesday, six hundred, bay four.” He stops. “That’s it. That’s the whole line.”

“What’s it for,” Denic says.

“It doesn’t say,” Corw says.

“There’s got to be a second half,” Denic says.

“There’s no second half,” Corw says. “Six names, a room and a time. Nobody’s written a word about what any of you did.” He goes back up the column and down it again. “Four third years, you, and somebody in the other block. That’s the year.”

Denic stands there and reads it himself, and what that comes to is his own name, six times.

“Somebody should have said,” Denic says.

“It went up Wednesday,” Corw says.

“Then somebody should have said on Wednesday,” Denic says.

“Nobody says,” Corw says. “There’s one instructor for two thousand of us and she was on the field this morning putting a canopy up. It goes on a board.” He’s already moving down the rows. “Where’s Calder.”

“I’m here,” I say.

“I mean where’s Calder on the board,” Corw says, and finds it, and stops. Then he goes along it with the finger and stops again.

“Say the number,” Corw says.

“I don’t know the number,” I say.

“I know you don’t know the number,” Corw says. “Fourteen thousand, six hundred and forty.”

Denic stops reading his own name.

“That’s since January,” Corw says. “That’s what you’ve pulled out of this place in five months and a bit.”

“All right,” I say.

“That is not all right,” Corw says, and moves the finger one column over, and puts it flat down on the surface this time. “Nothing.”

“Nothing what,” Denic says.

“Spent,” Corw says. “That column is spent. His says nothing.”

He turns around and puts his back against the board. All year he has talked about numbers with his eyes still on them and he isn’t doing that now.

“Sixteen and a half thousand,” Corw says. “That’s mine. Spent, all of it, the week I earned it, every time since January. I’m carrying four hundred and six.”

“Then he’s got more than you,” Denic says.

“He’s got thirty-six times what I’ve got,” Corw says.

“That’s good for him,” Denic says.

“It’s not just good for him,” Corw says. “I’ve been buying since week two. Everything I’m standing in, everything I’m walking on, all of it came out of that column and it’s all still on me.” He gets a hand out at shoulder height and drops it. “He’s been sitting on the whole of it since January like it’s a coat he hasn’t taken off.”

“Maybe he likes the coat,” Denic says.

“Nobody likes the coat,” Corw says, and pushes off the board and goes, and gets four steps and comes back. “Fourteen thousand six hundred and forty. Say it once so it’s in your head.”

“Fourteen thousand six hundred and forty,” I say.

“Good,” Corw says, and goes for real.

The bay’s hot by evening now that it’s June, both doors wedged and the fans on the ceiling turning slow enough to watch. Somebody down the hall still has the compressor that cuts out every forty seconds.

Doyle’s bench has Denic’s bracket up on it, out of the frame entirely, and the notch spacing has been redone in a way I can see from the door because the new marks are bright against the old ones. There are pads out beside it, four of them, the long kind, and a collar liner still in its sleeve, and the tools are laid along the back edge in the order he’ll want them.

Denic gets to the bench and stops.

“What’s all that,” Denic says.

“Your seat,” Doyle says.

“My seat’s fine,” Denic says.

“Your seat’s fine for four hours,” Doyle says. He’s got the bracket up against the light, sighting down it. “It’s not fine for six. At four hours you’re hurting and it doesn’t matter because you’re getting out. At six it’s in your hip and it’s in the hip the whole back half of it, and you don’t notice it going in, you notice it when your left hand starts arriving late and you think that’s your hand.”

“How do you know it’s six hours,” Denic says.

“It says extended duration,” Doyle says.

“That doesn’t say six,” Denic says.

“Third years do six,” Doyle says. “It’s on their rota and their rota’s on the same board.” He puts the bracket down and squares it to the edge of the bench.

Denic stands there.

“You read the board Wednesday,” Denic says.

“I read the board Wednesday,” Doyle says.

“You’ve known since Wednesday and you didn’t say anything to me,” Denic says.

“I did this instead,” Doyle says, and picks up the smaller driver and gets back into the bracket, and that’s the last he has to say about it.

Denic looks at the four pads and the liner in its sleeve for a while.

“Corw says four of them are third years,” Denic says.

“Four of them are third years,” Doyle says.

“You knew that too,” Denic says.

“I read the whole board,” Doyle says. “That’s what reading a board is.”

Fenn comes down the aisle with a coupling in one hand and looks at the bench and doesn’t say anything about it either.

“Six hours,” Fenn says.

“Six hours,” Doyle says.

“He’ll talk for all six,” Fenn says.

“He’ll talk for four,” Doyle says. “The fifth one he goes quiet and that’s the one I’d want to see.”

Reqa’s at our own bench with a housing open and the rag over her knee, and she doesn’t look up when I come down the aisle and she does look up when I sit down, and every other night of this year it has gone the other way round.

“Right side,” Reqa says.

“It’s closed,” I say.

“I know it’s closed,” Reqa says. “Everything’s closed. That’s not what I asked.”

“You didn’t ask,” I say.

“I said right side,” Reqa says.

She’s got both hands stopped on the housing and she’s watching my mouth while I talk, and I give her the shape of it, the standing still and the turn coming back the wrong way and the lift.

“Four legs,” Reqa says.

“Four legs,” I say.

“How,” Reqa asks.

“He got under it,” I answer.

“Then your arm was high,” Reqa says, and picks the driver up again. “Not wrong. High. That rail’s been sitting you back a finger since the wear got into it in April, and a finger back is a finger of arm you have to find again somewhere.”

“It didn’t feel high,” I say.

“It never does,” Reqa says. “Bring the rig down Sunday and I’ll put a gauge on it instead of guessing it off you.”

Ilyen’s two benches over with her back to us and she doesn’t turn around.

“It wasn’t the arm,” Ilyen says.

“What?” I say, and turn enough on the bench to get the back of her in view.

“He stood still,” Ilyen says. “You had nothing to work off. That’s all that was.”

“Standing still is doing something,” Denic says, from Doyle’s bench, with none of it in front of him.

“That’s what I said,” Ilyen says.

“That’s not what you said,” Denic says.

Ilyen doesn’t answer that, and goes back to whatever’s in front of her, and Reqa’s already got the gauge out of the drawer and is setting it flat against the housing to check it still reads zero.

My hand goes to my own side once, the seam that isn’t there anymore, checking a hit that’s a whole day old and already gone. I catch it doing that and put both hands back on the bench.

Down the aisle Denic has got the collar liner out of its sleeve and is holding it up against his own neck to see how it sits, which is four days early and no use to anybody, and Doyle lets him do it.

I get my kit off the hook and the wall panel by the door reads an hour and fifty to lights-out.

“Straps,” Reqa says, not looking up.

“I’ve got mine,” I say.

“I know,” Reqa says.

Fifteen tonight. I have it off the shape of the near squares before I’m through the doors.

Up. Four straps.

The boy about two years older is down here again and he has me in under thirty, and the one after him has me quicker than that, and both of them go the same way. My arm sits high the whole time. Somebody told me it was high this evening and now it’s the only part of me I can hear.

The third one I stop listening to it and take him in about six seconds.

Then I come off, with the lights still on over the near squares and most of half an hour left in them.

The common room’s down to about a dozen by the time I get up there. Fenn’s got her feet up on the bench end and a coupling in her lap she isn’t on the clock for, and Corw’s standing over her with the band up, back on the argument the two of them have been running since April.

“You still haven’t done the math,” Corw says.

“I did the math,” Fenn says.

“Then do it here,” Corw says.

Fenn puts the coupling down in her lap and holds her hand out, and Corw looks at the hand for a second before he works out what she wants and gives her the stylus off his own board.

She does it on the back of somebody’s dropped notice, small, four lines, and turns it around and holds it up at him without saying anything.

Corw reads it twice.

“That’s not the rate I had,” Corw says.

“No,” Fenn says.

“Where’d you get April,” Corw says.

“Off the board, same as you,” Fenn says. “I got it in April. That’s what recently meant.”

Corw takes the notice off her and folds it in half and puts it inside the front of his jacket, and doesn’t start it up again, and doesn’t ask for the stylus back either.

“That’s two months,” Corw says.

“It’s two months,” Fenn says.

Reqa’s on the bench end with my fourth strap flat across her knee and the buckle already off it. She didn’t ask me for it. It was in my kit an hour ago.

“How many,” Reqa says.

“Three,” I say.

“Good ones or bad ones,” Reqa says.

“One good,” I say.

“Out of three,” Reqa says.

“Out of three,” I say.

“Then it’s one,” Reqa says, and puts the buckle back through the fold and runs it flat with her thumb, and goes back to it.
